module

public import ToMathlib.InformationTheory.Coding.DualCode
public import ToMathlib.InformationTheory.Coding.GeneratorMatrix

/-!
# Weak self-duality of a code from its generator matrix

A **generator matrix** `G` encodes a message column vector `x` as the codeword `G *ᵥ x`, so the code
it generates is the range of the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` (`Matrix.mulVecLin`), i.e. the span of
the columns of `G` (Nielsen & Chuang, *Quantum Computation and Quantum Information*, §10.4.1 — the
convention of `ToMathlib.InformationTheory.Coding.GeneratorMatrix`). The code is **weakly
self-dual** (self-orthogonal, `LinearCode.IsSelfOrthogonal`) when every two codewords are orthogonal
under the standard dot product, `C ⊆ C⊥`.

This file proves **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.24** (p. 450): *a code with generator matrix `G` is
weakly self-dual if and only if `Gᵀ G = 0`.*

## Main results

* `LinearCode.isSelfOrthogonal_range_mulVecLin_iff`: the code `range G.mulVecLin` generated by a
  matrix `G` is weakly self-dual iff the Gram matrix `Gᵀ * G` vanishes.

## Implementation notes

The heart of the proof is the Gram identity `(G *ᵥ x) ⬝ᵥ (G *ᵥ y) = ((Gᵀ * G) *ᵥ x) ⬝ᵥ y`,
which turns the dot product of two codewords into a bilinear form of the messages driven by the Gram
matrix `Gᵀ * G`. Weak self-duality unfolds (`LinearCode.isSelfOrthogonal_iff`) to *all pairs of
codewords are orthogonal*, i.e. `∀ x y, (G *ᵥ x) ⬝ᵥ (G *ᵥ y) = 0`; through the Gram identity this is
`∀ x y, ((Gᵀ * G) *ᵥ x) ⬝ᵥ y = 0`, which holds exactly when `Gᵀ * G = 0` — the reverse direction is
immediate, the forward direction reads off each entry `(Gᵀ * G) i j` by feeding in the standard
basis vectors `Pi.single i 1`, `Pi.single j 1`.

The coefficients range over an arbitrary field `K`; N&C's binary codes are the case `K = ZMod 2`.
Note `Gᵀ * G = 0` is *not* `G = 0`: over a field of characteristic `2` a nonzero column `g` can be
self-orthogonal (`g ⬝ᵥ g = 0`), which is precisely why weakly self-dual binary codes exist and
underpin the CSS construction.
-/

open Matrix

@[expose] public section

namespace LinearCode

variable {K m n : Type*} [Field K] [Fintype m] [Fintype n] [DecidableEq m]

/-- **Nielsen & Chuang, Exercise 10.24** (p. 450): the code generated by a matrix `G` — the range of
the encoding map `x ↦ G *ᵥ x` (`Matrix.mulVecLin`), i.e. the column span of `G` — is **weakly
self-dual** (self-orthogonal, `C ⊆ C⊥`) if and only if the Gram matrix `Gᵀ * G` vanishes.

The `(Gᵀ * G) i j` entry is the dot product of columns `i` and `j` of `G`, so `Gᵀ * G = 0` says
exactly that the columns of `G` — a spanning set of codewords — are pairwise orthogonal (including
each with itself), which is equivalent to orthogonality of all codewords. -/
theorem isSelfOrthogonal_range_mulVecLin_iff (G : Matrix m n K) :
    IsSelfOrthogonal (LinearMap.range G.mulVecLin) ↔ Gᵀ * G = 0 := by
  classical
  -- The Gram identity: dotting two codewords is the bilinear form of the messages via `Gᵀ * G`.
  have key : ∀ x y : n → K, (G *ᵥ x) ⬝ᵥ (G *ᵥ y) = ((Gᵀ * G) *ᵥ x) ⬝ᵥ y := fun x y => by
    rw [Matrix.dotProduct_mulVec, ← Matrix.mulVec_transpose, Matrix.mulVec_mulVec]
  rw [isSelfOrthogonal_iff]
  constructor
  · -- Forward: read off each entry `(Gᵀ * G) i j` with standard basis messages.
    intro h
    ext i j
    have hij := h (G.mulVecLin (Pi.single j 1)) (LinearMap.mem_range_self _ _)
      (G.mulVecLin (Pi.single i 1)) (LinearMap.mem_range_self _ _)
    rw [Matrix.mulVecLin_apply, Matrix.mulVecLin_apply, key] at hij
    simpa [Matrix.mulVec_single, dotProduct_single] using hij
  · -- Reverse: `Gᵀ * G = 0` kills the bilinear form for every pair of codewords.
    intro h x hx y hy
    obtain ⟨a, rfl⟩ := hx
    obtain ⟨b, rfl⟩ := hy
    rw [Matrix.mulVecLin_apply, Matrix.mulVecLin_apply, key, h, Matrix.zero_mulVec,
      zero_dotProduct]

end LinearCode

end
